  "manuscript": [
    {
      "id": "cmfh7otw1e4",
      "title": "I. The Light",
      "synopsis": "",
      "notes": "",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "sjr2oh4w1e4",
          "title": "Last of the oil",
          "status": "revised",
          "synopsis": "Wren measures the paraffin and does the arithmetic she has been avoiding.",
          "pov": "ch0hhy0yw1e4",
          "characters": [
            "ch0hhy0yw1e4"
          ],
          "location": "lof40c3nw1e4",
          "threads": [
            "t5fm1ugw1e4",
            "te3x9xlw1e4"
          ],
          "when": "Day 1, dusk",
          "notes": "",
          "content": "<p>There were eleven cans left in the oil store, and Wren counted them twice because the first time she had come up with twelve and wanted to be wrong.</p><p>Eleven cans. Four nights to a can, if she trimmed the wick the way her father had shown her, low and blue and miserly. Forty-four nights. The relief keeper was due in thirty. She had told herself that so often it had worn smooth, like a stone she kept in her pocket.</p><p>Above her the lens turned on its bath of mercury, patient as a clock, throwing its long arm out across the water to <em>nobody</em>. That was the part no one in Port Caddow understood. You did not keep the light for the ships you could see.</p><p>She shut the store, climbed the hundred and fourteen steps, and lit the lamp at six minutes past four, exactly as the tables said.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "stfa8b1w1e4",
          "title": "A boat in the fog",
          "status": "draft",
          "synopsis": "A rowing boat with no oars drifts onto the rocks. There is a man in it, and he is not cold.",
          "pov": "ch0hhy0yw1e4",
          "characters": [
            "ch0hhy0yw1e4",
            "chpee5wiw1e4"
          ],
          "location": "lof40c3nw1e4",
          "threads": [
            "tajz2bmw1e4"
          ],
          "when": "Day 2, before dawn",
          "notes": "",
          "content": "<p>The fog came in before dawn the way it always did in October, not rolling but simply <em>arriving</em>, as if the sea had breathed out.</p><p>Wren heard the boat before she saw it: wood on rock, a slow grinding knock, like someone at a door who knows they will be let in.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmm9tjnw1e4",
      "title": "II. The Harbour",
      "synopsis": "",
      "notes": "",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "sh56sakw1e4",
          "title": "Ansel’s warning",
          "status": "draft",
          "synopsis": "The harbourmaster rows out to tell Wren the Trinity Board means to shut the light. She tells him about the stranger instead. Mistake.",
          "pov": "ch0hhy0yw1e4",
          "characters": [
            "ch0hhy0yw1e4",
            "chz4r429w1e4"
          ],
